WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.200
- [Instructor] In this
video, we're going to review

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:05.220
what we've already learned
about monopsony employers,

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.560
that we've covered in a previous video,

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.210
but then we're going to add a twist

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.410
of adding a minimum wage
and see what happens.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:14.360
And it's actually interesting.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.860
It's actually somewhat counterintuitive.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.270
So just as a bit of a review,

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:21.120
when we're talking about
a monopsony generally,

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.400
we're talking about one buyer and many,

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:26.930
many sellers.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:30.060
So when we're talking
about a monopsony employer,

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:34.090
that one buyer is the one
employer that might be,

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:36.780
say, in a small town, and
they're the big industry there.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.330
And they're the only person

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:39.910
that a lot of folks could work for.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:41.320
And then the many sellers,

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:42.760
well, those are all of the workers,

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:45.260
the people who are selling their labor.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:50.000
And we've already talked
about the rational quantity

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.640
of labor that that monopsony
employer would hire.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.500
So you have your wage,

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.150
and on that axis, that's in dollars.

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:02.300
You have your quantity of labor

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.060
on the horizontal axis.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:05.690
We could think about our downward-sloping

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.610
marginal revenue product of labor.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:10.540
Every incremental unit of labor,

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:14.880
you might get a little bit
lower and lower marginal revenue

00:01:16.298 --> 00:01:18.150
from that input.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:21.550
So this is our marginal
revenue product, MRP.

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:23.670
We could call it marginal
revenue product of labor

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.650
'cause you could also have
marginal revenue product

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.840
of capital or of land,
other factors of production.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.000
But we're talking about employment here.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.380
And then we could think about
the marginal factor cost.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.260
Now, this is a significant difference

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:38.050
between a monopsony employer

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:41.430
and an employer in a perfectly
competitive labor market.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:42.530
If we were dealing with a perfectly

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.020
competitive labor market,

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.370
then the employer would just
have to pay the market wage.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.180
And so the marginal factor cost,

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:50.330
the amount that they would have to pay

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:53.980
for an incremental unit of
labor, will just be a constant,

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.560
whatever the market wage of labor is.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.840
So that would be the marginal factor cost.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:00.020
And it would be rational,

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:02.410
if this was not a monopsony employer,

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.130
to just keep hiring more and more,

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:05.740
as long as the marginal revenue product

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:06.960
is higher than the marginal,

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.170
this flat marginal factor cost curve,

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:11.150
which is set by the market wage rate.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:13.810
So it would produce or it
would hire, I should say,

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.620
this quantity of labor.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:17.470
But for a monopsony employer,

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.840
you could view them as, in
a lot of ways, they are the,

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.200
the labor market is,

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.800
they are the only buyer
in the labor market.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.260
And so we could think about
a supply curve of labor

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.910
just for their market or
just for their industry

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:34.680
or just for them really.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.650
And so it could look something like this.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:39.940
So this is the supply of labor,

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:41.810
supply,

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:45.670
supply of labor.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.300
And we've talked about that this is not

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.750
the marginal factor cost curve
for the monopsony employer.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.220
Because even though that
first person might be willing

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.670
to work for this wage and then
the very next unit of labor

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:59.010
might be willing to work
for a slightly higher wage,

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:00.730
if you have to give that
slightly higher wage

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:02.840
to the second person, we
assume that whatever wage

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.400
they give to one person they
have to give to everyone.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.310
In this video, we're going to review

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:11.500
what we know about a monopsony employer,

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.360
and then we're going to add a twist

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.710
that deals with minimum
wages, which are going to be,

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:17.920
it's going to be a little
bit counterintuitive.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.090
So just as a review, a
monopsony in general is

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.540
when you have one buyer and many sellers.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:27.720
And a monopsony employer is the situation

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.640
where that one buyer,
they're the buyer of labor,

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.120
of that factor of production.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.870
And so you have one employer.

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:36.180
And then the many sellers,
these are the sellers of labor,

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.850
the people who are
providing their own labor.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:40.110
So these are all of the workers.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:42.500
And we've already studied
this in previous videos,

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.260
but I will review it right now.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.900
We can draw our classic axes

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.440
where you have your wage rate here

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.790
in dollars on the vertical axis.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:56.450
You have your quantity of labor

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:58.500
right there in the horizontal axis.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.940
We can think about our
marginal revenue product

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:03.520
of labor curve.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:06.200
So every incremental unit of labor,

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:11.200
the firm gets a little
bit less benefit in,

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:16.140
measured by revenue, so less
and less and less revenue.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.830
And so this is marginal revenue product.

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:20.410
This is marginal revenue product of labor

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:22.160
'cause we're talking about labor markets.

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:23.640
If this was another factor of production,

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.020
it would be marginal
revenue product of capital

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:27.810
or of land or whatever else.

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.310
And then we could think about
the marginal factor cost.

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:33.130
If this were not a monopsony employer,

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:34.930
well, then the marginal
factor cost would just be

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:37.880
the market wage rate, so it
would look something like that.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:42.210
And we've already seen this,
if this was an employer

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:43.960
in a perfectly competitive labor market.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:45.660
But this is not an employer

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.010
in a perfectly competitive labor market.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:50.440
They're the only employer
in this small town.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:52.480
They're the big industry,
and so they're going

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.200
to have their own unique
labor supply curve.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.670
So it might look something like this,

00:04:58.670 --> 00:04:59.763
labor,

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:02.690
I'll just write supply of labor,

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:05.620
supply of labor.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:07.090
And you might be tempted,

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.330
and we thought about this in
previous video, to say, hey,

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:14.000
this is going to be the marginal
factor cost curve of labor.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.020
'Cause you say, well, you know,

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:17.450
that first person is willing
to work for that much,

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:20.210
the second person is willing
to work for that much,

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:21.690
or the second unit of labor.

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:24.200
But remember, we said or
we're making the assumption

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:26.350
that even a monopsony
employer, if it pays,

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:27.670
if it has to raise the wage

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.300
to get that second unit of labor,

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:32.020
it has to raise the wage for everyone.

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:35.160
So when it goes further and
further up the supply curve,

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:37.140
as it hires more and more and more,

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:39.080
it's not just paying
the incremental amount

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:42.070
to that incremental person,
it has to pay it to everyone.

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:46.150
So its marginal factor cost is
going to go up twice as fast.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:48.210
So the marginal factor cost curve

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:50.570
is going to look something like this.

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:53.090
So it's going to look like this,

00:05:53.090 --> 00:05:56.050
our marginal factor cost curve.

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:56.883
And this is, of course,

00:05:56.883 --> 00:05:58.970
when we're dealing with
a monopsony employer.

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:01.210
And so what is the
rational quantity of labor

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:03.180
for this firm to hire?

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:06.290
Well, it's going to keep hiring as long as

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:09.670
on the margin, every incremental
unit of labor it brings on,

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:12.550
it gets more revenue than
it has to incur in cost.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:15.210
So as long as the marginal
revenue product is higher

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:17.710
than the marginal factor
cost, it keeps hiring

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:19.970
all the way until this
point when they're equal.

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:21.840
So it would hire,

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:24.570
it would hire this quantity of labor.

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:27.100
And what is the wage it would have to pay?

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.420
Sometimes we just instinctively
go back to this dot.

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:31.480
We just want to go horizontally
'cause we're thinking about

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:34.050
thinking about equilibrium
prices and quantities.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:36.160
But remember, this, at this quantity,

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:37.860
this is not the wage up here,

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:40.350
the wage is defined by the supply curve.

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:44.390
And so this is the wage
that the firm would pay.

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:46.920
Now I'm going to add a twist.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:50.390
What do you think would happen
if this town said, all right,

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:52.880
well, this employer has
too much power here,

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:55.650
and we think they should pay higher wages.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:59.630
What do you think would happen
if the town increased the,

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:04.630
or enacted a minimum wage
that is higher than W sub one?

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:06.880
And actually, let's assume a situation

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:09.150
where the town enacts a minimum wage

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:12.630
that's right here at W sub two.

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:14.860
What does the marginal factor cost curve

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:17.800
now look like for this monopsony employer?

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:20.510
And now what would be
the rational quantity

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:22.230
of labor to employ?

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:24.080
Pause this video, and think about it.

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:28.680
Well, in a world where the
government sets a minimum wage,

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.110
well, then it starts to
feel, at least graphically,

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:33.270
a lot more like a
competitive labor market.

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:34.980
Although, this isn't due to market forces,

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:37.690
this is due to just some
type of government law.

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:40.610
But then the monopsony
employer will no longer

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:44.710
have its own supply of
labor curve like this,

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:47.060
and it would no longer
have this upward-sloping

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:48.520
marginal factor cost curve.

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:50.930
I'll call this marginal
factor cost curve one.

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:53.940
Now the government has defined the wage.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.090
So no matter how many people they hire,

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:57.750
they're going to pay that same wage.

00:07:57.750 --> 00:08:00.150
So the marginal factor
cost is now going to be

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:05.150
a horizontal line at that
wage that the government set,

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:08.140
so the marginal factor cost sub two,

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:10.200
and this is due to a

00:08:11.733 --> 00:08:12.816
minimum wage.

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:18.040
Now, what is the rational
quantity to hire?

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:20.280
Well, like always, it's rational

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:22.360
to keep hiring as long as you're on,

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:24.330
at every incremental unit of labor,

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:26.040
your marginal revenue product is higher

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:27.900
than your marginal factor cost.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:30.760
So they keep hiring all
the way until this point,

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:33.780
and so this is the rational
quantity for them to hire.

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:35.050
Let's call that Q sub two.

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:36.290
And we already know the wage,

00:08:36.290 --> 00:08:38.980
that's the minimum wage
that the government has set.

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:41.880
Now, something very
counterintuitive has happened.

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:43.360
Now, all of a sudden, it's rational

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:46.580
for the firm to hire more people.

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:48.400
And the reason why I say
it's counterintuitive,

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:50.480
if I'm just running
some type of a business

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:53.000
and all of a sudden I
have to pay higher wages,

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.130
well, I might say, well,
gee, I can't afford

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:57.110
to pay as many people, so I'm not going,

00:08:57.110 --> 00:08:59.920
I actually might have
to reduce my employment.

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:01.240
And that might be the case

00:09:02.742 --> 00:09:04.990
if we're dealing with other
types of labor markets,

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:07.100
if I'm not a monopsony employer.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:08.560
But at least theoretically,

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:10.450
based on all the assumptions we've made,

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:12.830
and we can debate on
how transferable this is

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:14.080
to the real world,

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:17.110
if you have a monopsony
employer, what this did is,

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:21.670
is this actually caused
the employer to hire more.

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:23.880
And you might say why is that?

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:27.120
Well, now the employer
is paying the same wage

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:28.810
regardless of how many people they hire.

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:30.870
Before, every incremental
person they hired,

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:32.380
every incremental unit of labor,

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:34.460
it raised the wage for everyone.

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:37.340
And so in some ways or in a very real way,

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:40.580
this made it rational for
the firm to hire more.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:41.840
Now, that doesn't mean that the firm

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:43.720
is better off in this situation.

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:46.003
It's just splitting the,

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:50.760
it's just splitting that
marginal revenue product

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:54.940
differently between the
firm and the employers.

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:56.540
In the old situation,

00:09:56.540 --> 00:10:01.540
the firm was able to capture
everything above the wage line

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:03.990
and below the marginal
revenue product line.

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:07.290
So the firm was able
to capture all of that.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:08.810
While, in this new world,

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:13.470
the firm is capturing
everything above the wage line

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:15.600
and below the marginal
revenue product line.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:18.740
So it is a different area.

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:20.940
But the other thing to keep in mind is

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:23.420
I picked a very particular wage

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:26.740
that would cause this horizontal
marginal factor cost curve

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:29.340
to intersect the marginal
revenue product curve

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:32.020
right where the marginal
revenue product curve

00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:34.470
intersects the supply of labor curve.

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:36.010
I could've picked another wage.

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:38.810
So the government could have
overshot, and it could've said,

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:40.340
hey, we're gonna raise the minimum wage

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:42.440
up here to W sub three.

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:43.870
And then what would happen?

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:48.310
Well, then your marginal factor
cost curve would be up here,

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:50.470
marginal factor cost sub three,

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:54.630
and the rational quantity to
hire would actually go down,

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:56.450
which is a little bit more intuitive

00:10:56.450 --> 00:10:59.120
for what would happen when
you enact a minimum wage.

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:00.250
But at least theoretically,

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:02.380
when you're dealing with
a monopsony employer,

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:05.830
there could be a situation
where forcing the wages up

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:08.340
could cause the employer to hire more,

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:09.640
which is counterintuitive.

